   "source": [
    "first_answer = f\"(1) The final test accuracy was {history_rnn.test_acc[-1]*100:.2f}%.\"\n",
    "display(Markdown(first_answer))\n",
    "\n",
    "plot = px.line(history_rnn.get_dict(), x=torch.arange(1,6), y=[\"train_loss\", \"test_loss\"], labels={\"value\": \"Loss\", \"x\": \"Epoch\"})\n",
    "second_answer = \"(2) Yes the model shows signs of converging. After 5 epochs the train loss and test loss are both decreasing. Signs of overfitting may be present as test_loss and train_loss diverge. More epochs would be required to confirm.\"\n",
    "display(Markdown(second_answer))\n",
    "\n",
    "third_answer = \"\"\"(3) During training backpropagation is used to determine the rate at which the loss value changes with each weight in the network. For every nurmerical operation taken by the network, backpropagation will compute the partial gradient of the operation and multiply that with the backpropagated gradient. For long input sequences, the RNN has to recurse over the sequence and if partial gradient terms are less than 1 (or larger than 1) in magnitude, values shrink towards $0$ (or $\\\\infty$ (exploding gradients)). Ultimately this means early timesteps get almost no learning signal and the RNN will fail to learn long-range dependencies.\n",
    "\"\"\"\n",
    "display(Markdown(third_answer))\n",
    "\n",
    "plot.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "26454cb5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## PART 2. LSTM Sentiment Classifier: From RNN to LSTM\n",
    "\n",
    "The Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) adds gates that let the model decide what to remember and what to forget. In PyTorch, swapping an RNN for an LSTM requires only one line change — but the performance gain is substantial."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c6fc23df",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The key difference: <span style=\"font-family: 'JetBrians';\"> nn.LSTM </span> returns \n",
    "(output, (hidden, cell)) instead of (output, hidden). We use only the final hidden state for classification."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d3608f89-d999-4c8a-97e4-79339fbd9b8c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Task 2.1. DEFINE LSTM MODEL"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "id": "4f04ff91",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "LSTMClassifier(\n",
      "  (embedding): Embedding(20002, 128)\n",
      "  (lstm): LSTM(128, 256, num_layers=2, batch_first=True, dropout=0.5)\n",
      "  (fc): Linear(in_features=256, out_features=2, bias=True)\n",
      ")\n",
      "Epoch 1/5 Train Loss: 0.6276 | Train Acc: 0.6409 | Test Loss: 0.5714 | Test Acc: 0.7274\n",
      "Epoch 2/5 Train Loss: 0.5665 | Train Acc: 0.7066 | Test Loss: 0.4542 | Test Acc: 0.7939\n",
      "Epoch 3/5 Train Loss: 0.3913 | Train Acc: 0.8274 | Test Loss: 0.4155 | Test Acc: 0.8291\n",
      "Epoch 4/5 Train Loss: 0.2969 | Train Acc: 0.8765 | Test Loss: 0.3417 | Test Acc: 0.8522\n",
      "Epoch 5/5 Train Loss: 0.2366 | Train Acc: 0.9075 | Test Loss: 0.3189 | Test Acc: 0.8660\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "class LSTMClassifier(nn.Module):\n",
    "    def __init__(self, vocab_size, embed_dim, hidden_dim, n_classes,\n",
    "                 n_layers=2, dropout=0.5):\n",
    "        \"\"\" Your Code\"\"\"\n",
    "        super().__init__()\n",
    "        self.n_layers = n_layers\n",
    "        self.embedding = nn.Embedding(vocab_size, embed_dim)\n",
    "        self.lstm = nn.LSTM(embed_dim, hidden_dim, batch_first=True, num_layers=n_layers, dropout=dropout)\n",
    "        self.fc = nn.Linear(in_features=hidden_dim, out_features=n_classes)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # def forward(self, x):\n",
    "    def forward(self, x, lengths): # had to modify forward pass so that lengths can be used to extract the correct final hidden state\n",
    "        \"\"\" Your Code\"\"\"\n",
    "\n",
    "        embeddings = self.embedding(x)\n",
    "        o, (_, _) = self.lstm(embeddings)\n",
    "        \n",
    "        batch_size = x.size(0)\n",
    "        actual_last_outputs = o[torch.arange(batch_size), lengths - 1]\n",
    "        \n",
    "        return self.fc(actual_last_outputs)\n",
    "\n",
    "model_lstm = LSTMClassifier(\n",
    "    vocab_size = len(vocab),\n",
    "    embed_dim  = 128,\n",
    "    hidden_dim = 256,\n",
    "    n_classes  = 2,\n",
    "    n_layers   = 2\n",
    ")\n",
    "print(model_lstm)\n",
    "\n",
    "train_lstm = ModelTrainStats(\n",
    "    name=\"LSTM\",\n",
    "    model=model_lstm\n",
    ")\n",
    "models.add(train_lstm)\n",
    "\n",
    "history_lstm = run_training(train_lstm, train_loader, test_loader)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0cddc563",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### TASK 2.2. TRAIN THE LSTM\n",
    "\n",
    "Re-use your training loop from Part 1 to train model_lstm for 5 epochs with the same hyperparameters.\n",
    "\n",
    "***REQUIREMENTS***\n",
    "\n",
    "(1). Report final test accuracy.\n",
    "\n",
    "(2). On the same plot, show training loss curves for both the RNN and the LSTM. Label each line clearly.\n",
    "\n",
    "(3). Which model trains faster (loss drops quicker)? Why might that be?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "id": "8a12a664",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/markdown": [
       "(1) The final test accuracy was 86.60%."
      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "text/markdown": [
       "(2) See plot below"
      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "text/markdown": [
       "(3) The LSTM trains faster. The LSTM cell state creates a more direct pathway for information and gradients to flow across many time steps. In a simple RNN, gradients must repeatedly pass through recurrent weight matrices, making them more likely to vanish. In an LSTM, the forget gate can preserve relevant memory by keeping the cell state largely unchanged, allowing earlier sentiment cues to influence later predictions more effectively and allowing the model to converge quicker.\n"
      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>"
      ]
     },
