           "ticks": "outside",
           "title": {
            "standoff": 15
           },
           "zeroline": false,
           "zerolinecolor": "rgb(36,36,36)"
          }
         }
        },
        "xaxis": {
         "anchor": "y",
         "domain": [
          0,
          1
         ],
         "title": {
          "text": "Epoch"
         }
        },
        "yaxis": {
         "anchor": "x",
         "domain": [
          0,
          1
         ],
         "title": {
          "text": "Loss"
         }
        }
       }
      }
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "first_answer = f\"(1) The final test accuracy was {history_lstm.test_acc[-1]*100:.2f}%.\"\n",
    "display(Markdown(first_answer))\n",
    "\n",
    "plot_lines = {\"lstm_train_loss\": history_lstm.train_loss, \"rnn_train_loss\": history_rnn.train_loss}\n",
    "\n",
    "plot = px.line(plot_lines, x=torch.arange(1,6), y=[\"lstm_train_loss\", \"rnn_train_loss\"], labels={\"value\": \"Loss\", \"x\": \"Epoch\"})\n",
    "second_answer = \"(2) See plot below\"\n",
    "display(Markdown(second_answer))\n",
    "\n",
    "third_answer = \"\"\"(3) The LSTM trains faster. The LSTM cell state creates a more direct pathway for information and gradients to flow across many time steps. In a simple RNN, gradients must repeatedly pass through recurrent weight matrices, making them more likely to vanish. In an LSTM, the forget gate can preserve relevant memory by keeping the cell state largely unchanged, allowing earlier sentiment cues to influence later predictions more effectively and allowing the model to converge quicker.\n",
    "\"\"\"\n",
    "\n",
    "display(Markdown(third_answer))\n",
    "\n",
    "plot.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "151dd83f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## PART 3. Bidirectional LSTM\n",
    "\n",
    "A unidirectional LSTM only reads text left-to-right. A Bidirectional LSTM (BRNN) runs two LSTMs in parallel — one forward, one backward — and concatenates their hidden states. This gives every token access to both its past and its future context."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3f768b13",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Note: Because the two directions are concatenated, the final hidden dimension is 2 × hidden_dim. Adjust your classifier head accordingly."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "09a7ab16",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Only two changes from <span style=\"font-family: 'JetBrians';\"> LSTMClassifier:</span> \n",
    "add <span style=\"font-family: 'JetBrians';\"> bidirectional=True</span>  to <span style=\"font-family: 'JetBrians';\"> nn.LSTM</span>, and double the input size of <span style=\"font-family: 'JetBrians';\"> nn.Linear</span>."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fe7201d8-e53e-4016-b81e-97a9ae03c4a8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### TASK 3.1. DEFINE BRNN (B-LSTM)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "id": "08ea4327",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "BRNNClassifier(\n",
      "  (embedding): Embedding(20002, 128)\n",
      "  (lstm): LSTM(128, 256, num_layers=2, batch_first=True, dropout=0.5, bidirectional=True)\n",
      "  (fc): Linear(in_features=512, out_features=2, bias=True)\n",
      ")\n",
      "Epoch 1/5 Train Loss: 0.6090 | Train Acc: 0.6651 | Test Loss: 0.5036 | Test Acc: 0.7519\n",
      "Epoch 2/5 Train Loss: 0.4253 | Train Acc: 0.8040 | Test Loss: 0.3478 | Test Acc: 0.8499\n",
      "Epoch 3/5 Train Loss: 0.2876 | Train Acc: 0.8787 | Test Loss: 0.3124 | Test Acc: 0.8713\n",
      "Epoch 4/5 Train Loss: 0.2355 | Train Acc: 0.9070 | Test Loss: 0.3070 | Test Acc: 0.8726\n",
      "Epoch 5/5 Train Loss: 0.1805 | Train Acc: 0.9329 | Test Loss: 0.3183 | Test Acc: 0.8753\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "class BRNNClassifier(nn.Module):\n",
    "    def __init__(self, vocab_size, embed_dim, hidden_dim, n_classes,\n",
    "                 n_layers=2, dropout=0.5):\n",
    "        \"\"\" Your Code\"\"\"\n",
    "        super().__init__()\n",
    "        self.n_layers = n_layers\n",
    "        self.hidden_dim = hidden_dim\n",
    "        self.embedding = nn.Embedding(vocab_size, embed_dim)\n",
    "        self.lstm = nn.LSTM(embed_dim, hidden_dim, batch_first=True, num_layers=n_layers, dropout=dropout, bidirectional=True)\n",
    "        self.fc = nn.Linear(in_features=hidden_dim * 2, out_features=n_classes)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def forward(self, x, lengths):\n",
    "        \"\"\" Your Code\"\"\"\n",
    "        embeddings = self.embedding(x)\n",
    "        # o (batch, L, hidden_dim * 2)\n",
    "        o, (_, _) = self.lstm(embeddings) # last dim of O is concatenation of forward and backward pass outputs\n",
    "        batch_size = x.size(0)\n",
    "        forward_last_outputs = o[torch.arange(batch_size), lengths - 1, :self.hidden_dim] # (batch, hidden_dim)\n",
    "        backward_last_outputs = o[torch.arange(batch_size), 0, self.hidden_dim:] # (batch, hidden_dim)\n",
    "        actual_last_outputs = torch.cat([forward_last_outputs, backward_last_outputs], dim=1) # (batch, hidden_dim*2)\n",
    "        return self.fc(actual_last_outputs)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # I considered using pack padded sequence here because it makes the code significantly simpler but felt that in order to make the model comparison fair at the end of the document I shouldn't use it here.\n",
    "    # def forward(self, x, lengths):\n",
    "    #     embeddings = self.embedding(x)\n",
    "\n",
    "    #     packed = nn.utils.rnn.pack_padded_sequence(\n",
    "    #         embeddings,\n",
    "    #         lengths.cpu(),\n",
    "    #         batch_first=True,\n",
    "    #         enforce_sorted=False,\n",
    "    #     )\n",
    "\n",
    "    #     o, (h_n, _) = self.lstm(packed)\n",
    "\n",
    "    #     # h_n shape: (num_layers*2, batch, hidden_dim)\n",
    "    #     forward_last = h_n[-2] # (batch, hidden_dim)\n",
    "    #     backward_last = h_n[-1] # (batch, hidden_dim)\n",
    "\n",
    "    #     actual_last_outputs = torch.cat([forward_last, backward_last], dim=1)\n",
    "\n",
    "    #     return self.fc(actual_last_outputs)\n",
    "\n",
    "model_brnn = BRNNClassifier(\n",
    "    vocab_size = len(vocab),\n",
    "    embed_dim  = 128,\n",
    "    hidden_dim = 256,\n",
    "    n_classes  = 2,\n",
    "    n_layers   = 2\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(model_brnn)\n",
    "\n",
    "train_brnn = ModelTrainStats(\n",
    "    name=\"BRNN\",\n",
    "    model=model_brnn\n",
    ")\n",
    "models.add(train_brnn)\n",
    "\n",
    "history_brnn = run_training(train_brnn, train_loader, test_loader)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5b048b94",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### TASK 3.2. TRAIN THE BRNN\n",
    "\n",
    "Re-use your training loop from Part 1 to train model_brnn for 5 epochs with the same hyperparameters.\n",
    "\n",
    "***REQUIREMENTS***\n",
    "\n",
    "(1). Report final test accuracy.\n",
    "\n",
    "(2). On the same plot, show training loss curves for the RNN, the LSTM and the BRNN. Label each line clearly.\n",
    "\n",
    "(3). Which model trains faster (loss drops quicker)? Why might that be?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "id": "87e0be92",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "text/markdown": [
       "(1) The final test accuracy was 87.53%."
      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "text/markdown": [
       "(2) See plot below"
      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "text/markdown": [
       "(3) The BRNN trains faster. The BRNN is architecturally simpler (less complex gating architecture) than the LSTM and therefore may be quicker to converge. For the training set, sentiment classification may largely depend on local lexical cues (\"great\" vs \"horrible\") without any dependence on long-range dependencies meaning the LSTM's extra memory machinery may not provide much early benefit. Bidirectionality can also help because information is processed from both the start and end of the sequence, meaning some sentiment cues have a shorter path to the final representation. The FC layer of the BRNN is also twice the size of the LSTM which may aid convergence.\n"
      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "<IPython.core.display.Markdown object>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
