   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### TASK 4.2. LIVE PREDICTION FUNCTION\n",
    "\n",
    "Implement predict_sentiment(text, model, vocab, device) that takes a raw English string and returns \"Positive\" or \"Negative\" along with the confidence score. Test it on at least four reviews of your own invention (two obviously positive, two ambiguous)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "id": "3f4aad55-ac5b-4f24-9eb9-93775f7fd379",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "\"This is the worst thing to happen to Cats since Dogs\": Negative\n",
      "\"I enjoyed the first 10 minutes and last 10 minutes, but could have gone without the middle 90min\": Negative\n",
      "\"I can't remember the last time I watched an actor fail to walk into a room convincingly\": Negative\n",
      "\"This was a really good film\": Positive\n",
      "\"I wish the film never ended\": Positive\n",
      "\"MOVIE MOVIE MOVIE MOVIE\": Positive\n",
      "\"I like oranges\": Negative\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Your Code\n",
    "\n",
    "device = torch.device(\"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# I'm using the encode function set up in section 0.2 so the provided vocab argument is unused\n",
    "def predict_sentiment(text:str, model: nn.Module, vocab, device):\n",
    "    # the model is set up to process batched data so we make a batch of size 1\n",
    "    tokens = torch.tensor([encode(text)]).to(device)\n",
    "    model.to(device)\n",
    "    model.eval()\n",
    "\n",
    "    with torch.no_grad():\n",
    "        y = model(tokens, lengths=torch.tensor([tokens.size(1)], dtype=torch.long).to(device))\n",
    "\n",
    "    return \"Negative\" if y.argmax(dim=1).item() == 0 else \"Positive\"\n",
    "\n",
    "test_cases = [\n",
    "    \"This is the worst thing to happen to Cats since Dogs\", # obvious\n",
    "    \"I enjoyed the first 10 minutes and last 10 minutes, but could have gone without the middle 90min\", # obvious\n",
    "    \"I can't remember the last time I watched an actor fail to walk into a room convincingly\", # obvious\n",
    "    \"This was a really good film\", # obvious\n",
    "    \"I wish the film never ended\", # obvious\n",
    "    \"MOVIE MOVIE MOVIE MOVIE\", # ambiguous\n",
    "    \"I like oranges\", # ambiguous\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "for i in test_cases:\n",
    "    print(f\"\\\"{i}\\\": {predict_sentiment(i, model_brnn, vocab, device)}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f96e608b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### TASK 4.3. ERROR ANALYSIS\n",
    "\n",
    "Find 5 misclassified examples from the test set using your best model. For each, print the first 100 tokens of the review, the true label, and the predicted label.\n",
    "\n",
    "Identify at least two patterns that explain the errors. Common culprits: sarcasm, negation (\"not bad\"), domain-specific words, very long reviews."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 18,
   "id": "ea444c1b",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# This cell automatically finds the first 5 misclassified examples from the test set\n",
    "# It uses the provided test_loader. It should be noted however that the test_loader does not randomise\n",
    "# the order and therefore the first 12500 are all examples whose label is negative.\n",
    "\n",
    "inv_vocab = {v: k for k, v in vocab.items()}\n",
    "\n",
    "def decode(text):\n",
    "    out = \"\"\n",
    "    for i in text:\n",
    "        out += inv_vocab.get(i.item(), \"\") + \" \"\n",
    "    return out\n",
    "        \n",
    "def find_misclassifications(model: nn.Module, loader, device):\n",
    "    counter = 0\n",
    "    for texts, labels, lengths in test_loader:\n",
    "        texts, labels, lengths = texts.to(device), labels.to(device), lengths.to(device)\n",
    "\n",
    "        y_hat = model(texts, lengths)\n",
    "        _, pred = y_hat.max(axis=1)\n",
    "        incorrect_indices = (pred != labels).nonzero()\n",
    "\n",
    "        for i in incorrect_indices:\n",
    "            text = decode(texts[i, torch.arange(0, 100)])\n",
    "            classification = \"Negative\" if pred[i].item() == 0 else \"Positive\"\n",
    "            display(Markdown(f\"Misclassified example #{counter+1}. Incorrectly labelled as {classification} \\n> `{text}`\"))\n",
    "\n",
    "            counter += 1\n",
    "            if counter == 5: break\n",
    "        if counter == 5: break\n",
    "\n",
    "# I answered the question using the outputs from a brnn model I trained before I had fixed the PyTorch seed. I have copied the example outputs that I used into the markdown cell below.\n",
    "# find_misclassifications(model_brnn, test_loader, device)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "333d2f74",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Misclassified example #1. Incorrectly labelled as Positive \n",
    "> `worth the entertainment value of a rental especially if you like action movies this one features the usual car chases fights with the great van damme kick style shooting battles with the shell load shotgun and even terrorist style bombs all of this is entertaining and competently handled but there is nothing that really blows you away if you've seen your share before br br the plot is made interesting by the inclusion of a rabbit which is clever but hardly profound many of the characters are heavily stereotyped the angry veterans the terrified illegal aliens the crooked cops the`\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "Misclassified example #2. Incorrectly labelled as Positive \n",
    "> `first off let me say if you haven't enjoyed a van damme movie since <unk> you probably will not like this movie most of these movies may not have the best plots or best actors but i enjoy these kinds of movies for what they are this movie is much better than any of the movies the other action guys segal and dolph have thought about putting out the past few years van damme is good in the movie the movie is only worth watching to van damme fans it is not as good as wake of death which i`\n",
    "\n",
    "Misclassified example #3. Incorrectly labelled as Positive \n",
    "> `the forgotten aka don't look in the basement is a very cheaply made and very old looking horror movie br br the story is very slow and never really reaches anything worth getting excited about br br the patients at the asylum are embarrassingly funny especially sam and the old woman who always quotes an old saying to everyone look out for the bit when she gets close to the camera tell me you can watch without laughing br br now the gore is very poor looking with the blood looking pink in many scenes so it doesn't really deserve`\n",
    "\n",
    "Misclassified example #4. Incorrectly labelled as Positive \n",
    "> `four things intrigued me as to this film firstly it stars <unk> pope of popular fame who is always a pleasure to watch <unk> it features brilliant new zealand actress <unk> owen thirdly it is filmed in association with the new zealand film commission <unk> a friend recommended it to me however i was utterly disappointed the whole storyline is absurd and complicated with very little resolution <unk> acting is fine but owen is unfortunately under used the other actors and actresses are all okay but i am unfamiliar with them all aside from the nice <unk> which are <unk>`\n",
    "\n",
    "Misclassified example #5. Incorrectly labelled as Positive \n",
    "> `br br never ever take a film just for its good looking title br br although it all starts well the film suffers the same imperfections you see in b films its like at a certain moment the writer does not any more how to end the film so he ends it in a way nobody suspects it thinking this way he is ingenious br br a film to be listed on top of the garbage list br br <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad> <pad>`\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "**Identifying patterns to explain misclassifications**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Poor understanding of how a film may be good by the standards of its genre, but poor overall.\n",
    "\n",
    "    Example #1 describes the film as a worthwhile rental for the \"car chase fights\" class of film but later describes the film as overall not something that \"blows you away.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "    Example #2 describes positive elements to the film such as \"enjoy these kinds of movies\" and \"much better than any of the movies the other action guys\" but overall says that \"if you haven't enjoyed a van damme movie since you probably will not like this movie.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Lexical bias.\n",
    "\n",
    "    Both example #1 and #2 involve \"van damme\" as tokens. It is possible that there may have been a large number of positive reviews of the \"van damme\" film and the model has just learnt to associate a review with \"van damme\" as a positive review.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Poor identification of what is being described as good / bad.\n",
    "\n",
    "    Example #4 states the film \"stars pope of popular fame\" and \"features brilliant new zealand actress owen\" and \"a friend recommended it to me\" which are all positive descriptions, but aren't descriptions of the film itself. These positive sentiments likely pushed the model to view the review as positive when it should be negative.\n",
    "\n",
    "    Example #5 describes the film's title as good looking and that the film \"starts well\" and how the writer's think they are \"ingenius\" while the film itself is negatively reviewed."
   ]
  },
  {
